{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2615 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "912f9052060d1a9651e52dec69569aa6e3d8da28188d79d0a65ec43b16e0a026" :edb/db-before-t 2614 :edb/db-after-t 2615 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535927 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:48:52.599Z" 13194139535927 true] [17592186374677 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n=0,1,2,\\ldots.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R\\setminus\\{0\\}.$" 13194139535927 true] [17592186374677 1100 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" 13194139535927 false] [17592186374677 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535927 false] [17592186374677 1145 "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$\n\nIndexing and nonzero-amplitude clarification: In the positive-eigenvalue case above, $n=1,2,3,\\ldots$. The separate zero case contributes $\\lambda_0=0$ with $y_0(x)=B_0$, where $B_0\\ne0$. Thus the complete spectrum and eigenfunctions are $\\lambda_n=n^2/25$ and $y_n(x)=B_n\\cos(nx)$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ and $B_n\\ne0$. Since $5\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}=n$ for these indices, this is exactly the accepted displayed formula $B_n\\cos(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n})$. These explicit indices avoid assuming a convention for the source symbol $\\mathbb N$; a zero amplitude would produce the trivial solution, which is not an eigenfunction." 13194139535927 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
